# Difference-in-differences and the parallel trends assumption
next: {"title":"When treatment arrives at different times: staggered DiD in trouble","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/when-treatment-arrives-at-different-times-staggered-did-in-trouble.cast"}

## What the Double Difference Subtracts
voice: female
level: advanced
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    title: Uninsured rate, nonelderly adults
    row_label: Group
    col_label: Year
    row_values:
      - Expansion (D=1)
      - Non-expansion (D=0)
    col_values:
      - '2013'
      - '2014'
    cells:
      - [18.4%, 12.4%]
      - [22.7%, 19.0%]

Now that we have two groups and two periods on the board, let's work the arithmetic and ask exactly what the double difference subtracts.
    draw title grid row_title col_title

Rows are the groups — states that took the ACA Medicaid expansion in 2014 and states that refused it; columns are the year before and the year after.
    draw row_header_0 row_header_1 col_header_0 col_header_1 parallel true

In round American Community Survey numbers, about eighteen percent of nonelderly adults in expansion states were uninsured in 2013, and about twelve a year later.
    draw cell_0_0 cell_0_1

    pause 0.3

The non-expansion states started higher, near twenty-three percent, and by 2014 sat near nineteen.
    draw cell_1_0 cell_1_1

Read the treated row alone and you get a six-point fall — but 2014 also brought the exchanges, the individual mandate and a recovering labour market to every state in the country.
    highlight cell_0_0 cell_0_1 effect glow

So write the treated change down honestly: minus six points, cause and coincidence still mixed together.
    text diff_treated "Expansion: 12.4 − 18.4 = −6.0" #b5482e x 275 y 140 font_size 22

The control states got all of that same national weather, and fell three point seven points without expanding Medicaid at all.
    text diff_control "Non-expansion: 19.0 − 22.7 = −3.7" #2f6b8f x 720 y 140 font_size 22

    pause 0.4

Subtract the second change from the first, and about two and a half points survive — the double difference, and close to where published first-year estimates of the expansion's coverage effect land.
    text did_text "DiD = (−6.0) − (−3.7) = −2.3 points" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 62 font_size 26
    mark mark_did target did_text kind box
    delivery grave

    quiz "What is the 2×2 DiD estimator subtracting?" intro "A quick check."
        right: It is a difference of changes — minus six against minus three point seven — which nets out anything that hit both groups in 2014.
        wrong: Levels are not the object here; DiD works on changes over time.
        * The two groups' 2014 levels
        + The treated group's change minus the control group's change
        * The treated group's before-after change

Four numbers gave us one estimate. Nothing so far makes it causal.
    clear keep ["title"]

It becomes causal only under this: without treatment, the expected change in Y-of-zero, the untreated potential outcome, would have been the same in both groups.
    text assume_head "Parallel trends" x 500 y 580 font_size 34
    text eq_line1 "E[ Y_i(0)_2014 − Y_i(0)_2013 | D_i = 1 ]" #b5482e x 500 y 470 font_size 26
    text eq_line2 "=   E[ Y_i(0)_2014 − Y_i(0)_2013 | D_i = 0 ]" #2f6b8f x 500 y 400 font_size 26

And the boxed line is a fiction — what uninsurance in expansion states would have done in 2014 had they never expanded. That state of the world did not occur, so no dataset contains it.
    mark mark_unobs target eq_line1 kind box
    text unobs_note "the 2014 term here never happened" #8f887c x 500 y 338 font_size 20

    pause 0.4

Notice what the assumption never mentions: levels. The control states were four points worse insured at baseline, and a constant gap like that is differenced straight out.
    text note_levels "Levels may differ freely: 22.7% vs 18.4% in 2013" x 500 y 250 font_size 24

Parallel trends is a claim about slopes, not heights — which is why selection on the level of a state's uninsurance is harmless here, while selection on its trajectory is fatal.
    highlight eq_line1 eq_line2 effect glow

    quiz "Which quantity in the parallel-trends equation can never be observed?"
        right: The treated group's untreated 2014 outcome never happened — which is why parallel trends is an assumption and not a finding.
        wrong: That number is sitting in the data; the missing one is the counterfactual.
        * Expansion states' 2013 uninsured rate
        + Expansion states' 2014 rate had they not expanded
        * Non-expansion states' 2014 rate

The move is old, by the way: in the 1850s John Snow compared London households whose water company had moved its intake upstream of the sewage with neighbours whose company had not, before and after — a difference of differences, written long before anyone wrote E of Y-of-zero.

Difference-in-differences never claims the two groups are alike; it claims that, untreated, they would have moved alike.
    text final_line "Not alike — only moving alike" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 150 font_size 28

## Reading Pre-Trends Honestly
voice: female
level: advanced
use: event_study
with:
    periods_pre: 5
    periods_post: 5
    effect: 1
    ci: 0.55
    pre_trend: 0
    ramp: false
    y_label: Expansion − non-expansion gap
    title: Reading pre-trends honestly

Now that we have seen what the double difference subtracts, everything rests on one line we can never observe — so let's look hard at the picture researchers show instead.
    draw title axes zero_line

January 2014: about half the states expanded Medicaid under the ACA, and the rest did not.
    draw treat_line

    pause 0.3

Each dot is the gap between expansion and non-expansion states in one year, measured relative to the year before the expansion.
    draw coef_0 coef_1 coef_2 coef_3 coef_4 parallel true

That last pre-year sits exactly on zero because we put it there — it is normalization, not evidence.
    point at.ref coef_4 gesture circle

    pause 0.3

After 2014 the gap opens: coverage among low-income adults improves far more where Medicaid expanded, with published estimates clustering around five to six percentage points fewer uninsured.
    draw coef_5 coef_6 coef_7 coef_8 coef_9

Flat before, jumping after — this is the figure that is supposed to make you believe the design.
    focus coef_0 coef_1 coef_2 coef_3 coef_4

    pause 0.4

But parallel trends claims something about this half of the plot: that after 2014, the expansion states' untreated potential outcome, Y of zero, would have moved exactly like the non-expansion states'.
    focus coef_5 coef_6 coef_7 coef_8 coef_9

Those states with D set to zero after 2014 never existed, so not one dot on this plot observes the quantity the assumption is about.
    highlight treat_line effect glow

    quiz "Flat pre-treatment coefficients establish that…" intro "A quick check."
        right: They show only that the groups moved together beforehand — the assumption concerns the untreated path afterwards, which is never observed.
        wrong: Nothing measured before 2014 can observe the post-2014 counterfactual.
        * Parallel trends holds after treatment
        + The groups moved together before treatment
        * The treatment had no effect

And even as a test of the past, a failure to reject has two very different causes.
    camera center.ref coef_2 zoom 1.7

Suppose the gap was already drifting apart before anyone expanded anything — about an eighth of the eventual jump each year.
    animate pre_trend 0.12 duration 3

Every pre-estimate still touches zero; the confidence bands swallow the drift, and the test passes cleanly.
    highlight coef_0 coef_1 coef_2 coef_3 effect glow

Yet carried five years past 2014, that same invisible drift would manufacture more than half of the estimated effect.
    camera reset true

    quiz "Why is passing a pre-trend test weak reassurance in a noisy sample?"
        right: With wide intervals, a drift large enough to bias the estimate is easily missed, so passing tells you little.
        wrong: The intervals are wide, so a violation big enough to matter can slip through undetected.
        * Pre-trends are mechanically zero
        + The test has low power against violations that still matter
        * Confidence intervals shrink after treatment

Jonathan Roth's 2022 paper made this concrete: published event studies often have little power against trends big enough to overturn their results, and screening on flat pre-trends is itself a selection rule — you keep exactly the samples where noise happened to hide the slope.
    highlight coef_0 coef_1 coef_2 coef_3 coef_4 effect pulse

So the modern advice, from Rambachan and Roth, is to stop treating the pre-period as a hurdle to clear and start using it as a bound: ask how large a post-treatment violation the result could survive.

You got {score} of {score_total}. Flat pre-trends are a failed refutation, not a proof — they cannot see the counterfactual, and when the data are noisy they cannot even see the trend.
    delivery grave

## What DiD Buys You, and What It Borrows
voice: female
level: advanced
use: did_trends
with:
    effect: -1.5
    treat_at: 5
    group_labels:
      - Expansion states
      - Non-expansion states
    y_label: Mortality, low-income adults
    show_counterfactual: true
    title: Expansion vs non-expansion states, 2014

Now that we have seen how a faster state recovery could masquerade as a Medicaid effect, let's put the whole design on one figure: what difference-in-differences buys, and what it borrows.
    draw axes title

Non-expansion states — the group the policy never reached, measured before and after.
    draw control_line

    pause 0.3

Expansion states run alongside them until January 2014, and then their path bends downward.
    draw treated_line treat_line

    pause 0.3

Here is the borrowed piece: we take the control states' change and glue it onto the expansion states' own starting level.
    draw counterfactual

So the estimator subtracts two changes — the change among expansion states minus the change among non-expansion states — and calls the leftover the effect.
    draw effect_arrow

Parallel trends is a claim about that dashed line nobody observes: the average change in Y_i(0), the outcome with no expansion, would have been the same in both groups — levels may differ all they like, only the trend must match.
    highlight counterfactual effect glow delivery grave

    quiz "Parallel trends restricts which quantity?" intro "A quick check."
        right: It restricts the untreated potential outcome: both groups' Y(0) would have moved by the same amount.
        wrong: Levels are allowed to differ entirely — DiD differences them away; it is the untreated trend that must match.
        * The pre-period levels of Y
        + The trend in Y_i(0) in both groups
        * The size of the treatment effect

    clear

Question two: do flat lines before 2014 prove that assumption?
    text t2_claim "\"The pre-trends were flat, so the assumption holds\"" x 500 y 680 font_size 26

They only fail to refute it — the claim is about the years after the expansion, and no pre-period data can reach them.
    mark mark_claim target t2_claim kind strike

    quiz "Flat pre-2014 lines tell you that…"
        right: A passing pre-trend test refutes some bad stories; the assumption itself lives in the post period and stays untestable.
        wrong: The assumption concerns years the pre-period data never observe.
        * Parallel trends is verified
        + Some violations are ruled out, not all
        * The estimate is causal

Here is a story the test cannot touch: expansion states were disproportionately the ones whose economies rebounded faster, taking employment and income with them.
    text n_a "Faster rebound after 2013" #8a5fa8 x 300 y 570 font_size 24
    arrow arr_ab n_a -> n_b #8a5fa8
    text n_b "Employment and income rise" #8a5fa8 x 300 y 450 font_size 24

    pause 0.3

Mortality among low-income adults then falls in those states — and would have fallen with no expansion at all.
    arrow arr_bc n_b -> n_c #8a5fa8
    text n_c "Mortality falls anyway" #8a5fa8 x 300 y 330 font_size 24

The double difference cannot separate the two, so it books the whole gap as the Medicaid effect.
    arrow arr_cd n_c -> n_did #b5482e
    text n_did "DiD books it as Medicaid" #b5482e x 730 y 330 font_size 24
    mark mark_did target n_did kind box

And because that divergence begins in 2014, exactly with the policy, the pre-trend test sails straight through.
    text t2_note "The divergence starts in 2014 — the pre-trend test passes" #8f887c x 500 y 180 font_size 22

    quiz "Which story would break parallel trends here?"
        right: A recovery that starts with the policy moves the untreated path itself, and DiD credits that movement to Medicaid.
        wrong: Different starting levels are harmless — DiD differences them away; a different trend is what breaks it.
        * Expansion states started with higher mortality
        + Their economies rebounded faster from 2014
        * Enrollment took a few months to complete

    clear

So why use this design at all? Set it beside the study this course began with: Oregon drew about thirty thousand names from ninety thousand applicants, and bought identification outright — no assumption to defend, but only two years and a few thousand enrollees, far too little to see mortality.
    text c_title "Two designs, one question" x 500 y 690 font_size 28
    text n_lot "Oregon lottery, 2008" #2f6b8f x 260 y 590 font_size 26
    text lot_l1 "Randomized by design" x 260 y 535 font_size 21
    text lot_l2 "≈30,000 drawn, 2 years" x 260 y 495 font_size 21
    text lot_l3 "Mortality: too imprecise" x 260 y 455 font_size 21

The expansion comparison buys the opposite: every low-income adult in the country, followed for years — Miller, Johnson and Wherry find annual mortality down about a tenth of a percentage point, roughly nine percent — paid for with an assumption no data will ever verify.
    text n_did2 "ACA expansion DiD, 2014" #b5482e x 730 y 590 font_size 26
    text did_l1 "Parallel trends assumed" x 730 y 535 font_size 21
    text did_l2 "Millions of adults, many years" x 730 y 495 font_size 21
    text did_l3 "Mortality −0.13 pp/year (≈9%)" x 730 y 455 font_size 21

That is the trade this design makes: a lottery gives a credible answer to a small question, parallel trends lets you ask the national one, on borrowed credibility.
    text trade_text "Credibility  ⇄  statistical power" #8f887c x 500 y 350 font_size 26

Two groups, two periods, one subtraction — and the entire causal claim resting on a line that was never observed, which is why the defence of a DiD is never the regression, but the story about that line.
    text fin_text "The counterfactual is assumed, never observed" x 500 y 230 font_size 26
    mark mark_fin target fin_text kind box
    delivery grave

